// form-cli-router.fk — the ONE routing formula, as a recipe.
//
// preludes: form-stdlib/core.fk
//
// Every request the form-cli (or the MCP surface inside another agent) handles is
// compared across two backend descriptions. This scalar is a capability estimate,
// not permission to cross a membrane. There are two described backends:
//   form-native    a local Form recipe / trained lane (fully ours; ~0 cost)
//   agent-cli      the subscription agent CLI already running (claude/codex/...);
//                  finite credits/context, observable cost, never assumed paid
//
// The choice is ONE formula over four axes (each 0..100): sovereignty (how much
// this backend is ours), trust (its measured success rate on this shape),
// capability (can it do this category AT ALL — a hard gate), confidence (the
// local lane's accumulated certainty). Sovereignty and trust weigh highest — they
// are the core values; capability and confidence lift. The weights are a DATA
// cell (fcr-weights), tunable without touching the formula.
//
// The flywheel is emergent, not coded: form-native starts with capability 0 for
// the hard categories, so the formula routes to the agent CLI AND every such call
// is captured to train the local lane. As learning raises
// form-native's capability and confidence, its max-sovereignty advantage overtakes
// the agent CLI and the same request flips to local — the more we use the oracle,
// the less we need it.
//
// The axes are sovereignty (local 90 / remote 40) and trust (success rate);
// fcr-fitness names them as one explicit, tunable scalar for the per-request
// route.
//
// ONE confidence primitive. Confidence is a lane's measured pass-rate
// (passes*100/attempts, 0..100) — the certainty it has accumulated from real use,
// including oracle-taught attempts. fcr-confidence-axis is the SINGLE definition of
// confidence in the body: the router's fitness axis (fcr-confidence, fed by it) and
// any route-local-or-oracle gate read this one number from the same accumulated
// learning — so the per-request fitness scalar and that gate can never drift into
// two different notions of "confidence".
//
// THE confidence primitive: a lane's measured pass-rate (0..100) from real use.
// the fitness axis below is fed by it — one definition, read by both a route
// gate and the router fitness.
section [form.bml] {
    def fcr-confidence-axis(passes, attempts) = (Comparable) attempts == 0 ? 0 : passes * 100 / attempts;
    // the tunable weights: (w-sovereignty w-trust w-capability w-confidence divisor).
    // sovereignty and trust are the core values (weight 2); capability and confidence
    // lift (weight 1). Change the body's behavior by changing this data, not the math.
    def fcr-weights() = list(2, 2, 1, 1, 6);
    // a backend is (name, sovereignty, trust, capability, confidence).
    def fcr-backend(name, sovereignty, trust, capability, confidence) = list(name, sovereignty, trust, capability, confidence);
    def fcr-name(b) = nth(b, 0);
    def fcr-sovereignty(b) = nth(b, 1);
    def fcr-trust(b) = nth(b, 2);
    def fcr-capability(b) = nth(b, 3);
    def fcr-confidence(b) = nth(b, 4);
    // build a backend whose confidence axis is COMPUTED from accumulated learning by
    // the one confidence primitive — the same number a route gate reads.
    // This is the wire that makes the gate's input and the router fitness scalar
    // literally the same scalar: pass (passes attempts) once, both consume it.
    def fcr-backend-learned(name, sovereignty, trust, capability, passes, attempts) = fcr-backend(name, sovereignty, trust, capability, fcr-confidence-axis(passes, attempts));
    // THE one formula: a single fitness score from the four axes. Capability is a
    // hard gate — a backend that cannot do this category at all scores 0, whatever
    // its other axes. Among capable backends, the weighted sum decides.
    def fcr-fitness(b) = (Comparable) fcr-capability(b) == 0 ? 0 : (nth(fcr-weights(), 0) * fcr-sovereignty(b) + nth(fcr-weights(), 1) * fcr-trust(b) + (nth(fcr-weights(), 2) * fcr-capability(b) + nth(fcr-weights(), 3) * fcr-confidence(b))) / nth(fcr-weights(), 4);
    // Compare backend fitness; a tie goes to form-native. If this names agent-cli it
    // names a candidate capability only. form-cli-sufficiency's exact admission
    // membrane remains the sole authority for a frontier crossing.
    def fcr-route(form-native, agent-cli) = fcr-fitness(form-native) >= fcr-fitness(agent-cli) ? fcr-name(form-native) : fcr-name(agent-cli);
    // the flywheel made checkable: the SAME request, routed before vs after learning
    // raised form-native's capability/confidence, flips agent-cli -> form-native.
    def fcr-flips-local?(fn-before, fn-after, agent-cli) = (String) fcr-route(fn-before, agent-cli) == "agent-cli" && (String) fcr-route(fn-after, agent-cli) == "form-native" ? 1 : 0;
    // a route is sovereign when it landed on form-native (no external lane touched).
    def fcr-sovereign?(winner-name) = (String) winner-name == "form-native" ? 1 : 0;
    // THE healthy path, every request, one order:
    //   native — Form body / grounded cell
    //   local  — this machine's model (Metal, disk)
    //   remote — an unresolved frontier candidate, not crossing permission
    def fcr-lane-native() = 0;
    def fcr-lane-local() = 1;
    def fcr-lane-remote() = 2;
    def fcr-walk(native-hit, local-ready) = (Comparable) native-hit == 1 ? fcr-lane-native() : (Comparable) local-ready == 1 ? fcr-lane-local() : fcr-lane-remote();
}
